  "query_preview": "/FRIDAY NIGHT THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nIssued at 1236 AM MDT Thu Aug 27 2026\n\nSW flow aloft strengthens across CO on Sat, as center of the\nupper level high drifts back eastward into TX. Renewed tap of \nmid-level moisture will work into the state by late Sat\nafternoon, and with weak s/w trough moving across, expect fairly\nwidespread showers and storms to return to the mountains,\nespecially along the Continental Divide. Swly steering flow \nwill take a few storms into the I-25 corridor Sat evening, while\nthe plains stay mainly dry, as breezy sw surface winds behind \nthe lee trough in wrn KS aren't particularly helpful in keeping \nactivity going away from the higher terrain. Deep mixing Sat \nwill squeeze a couple more degf out of the air mass, with maxes \ninto the 90s on the plains (a few 100f readings in the lower \nArkansas Valley), and 80s mountains and valleys. Strong upper \nwave then races eastward through the nrn Rockies Sun/Mon, with \nsurface cold front dropping through the area late Sun/early Mon.\nMid level moisture will continue to flow across the state both \nSun/Mon, with widespread showers and thunderstorms over the \nmountains both days, lesser chances on the plains. Still hot on \nSun ahead of the front, then slightly cooler Mon/Tue behind it. \nPrecip chances may then decrease slightly Tue/Wed as main\nmoisture tap shifts back west of Colorado, though moisture \nnever goes away completely, so isolated afternoon/evening \nshowers and thunderstorms will persist, especially over the \nmountains back toward the Continental Divide.",
